ravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki","html_url":"https://github.com/jaki","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jaki/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2019-08-20T21:28:52Z","updated_at":"2019-08-20T21:28:52Z","body":"Creating more than one temporary table in a Postgres regress test seems to cause the next regress test in schedule to fail with a read RPC timeout.  I suspect that this issue captures the reason.  This seems to be an issue on CentOS but a non-issue on Mac.  Here is a minimal repro:\r\n* `repro_schedule`\r\n  ```\r\n  test: first\r\n  test: second\r\n  ```\r\n* `sql/first.sql`\r\n  ```\r\n  CREATE TEMP TABLE a (i int);\r\n  CREATE TEMP TABLE b (i int);\r\n  ```\r\n* `sql/second.sql`\r\n  ```\r\n  SELECT 1;\r\n  ```\r\n`results/first.out` would be identical to `expected/first.out`.  However, `results/second.out` would look like\r\n```\r\n ysqlsh: FATAL:  Timed out: Read RPC to **** timed out after 9.718s\r\n```\r\nwhich is not expected.\r\n\r\nIf the schedule had any more tests before and after, they wouldn't be affected.  Only `second` is affected because it is right after `first`.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/yugabyte/yugabyte-db/issues/comments/523202251/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"ev